comment:
Over-expression of human Cu, Zn-superoxide dismutase in motor neurons of Drosophila has been reported to extend lifespan up to 40% in wild-type flies. Expression of a human SOD1 transgene (HS) in Drosophila
motorneurons was achieved using the yeast GAL4/UAS system.
The mean (50% mortality) and maximum (90% mortality) lifespan for each genotype is as follows: HS1 (human SOD line 1) without D42-GAL4 (mean=45.1±3.4; max.=56.3±3.6); 
HS1 + D42-GAL4 (mean=63.7±4.3; max.=73.2±3.4; 
HS2 (human SOD line 2) without D42-GAL4 (mean=52.2±1.8; max.=58.8±1.5); 
HS2 + D42-GAL4 (mean=60.6±2.2; max.=71.0±2.7).
Elevated resistance to oxidative stress suggests that the lifespan extension observed in these flies is due to enhanced reactive oxygen metabolism.

